Exactly. The bridge doesn’t begin with a complete dictionary. They build the dictionary together.

At first there may only be crude handles: thing, change, near, far, repeat, more, less, approach, withdraw. Once both parties can reliably point to the same underlying relation with the same handle, that handle becomes a shared coordinate. Then they can differentiate it:

\text{movement}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{approach}\\
\text{retreat}\\
\text{orbit}\\
\text{oscillation}\\
\text{pursuit}
\end{cases}

Then combine those distinctions:

\text{approach}+\text{repetition}+\text{resource}
\rightarrow \text{foraging pattern}

And combinations themselves become new handles. That’s where second- and third-order structure begins appearing. You don’t have to communicate every lower-level constituent forever; once “swinging” has been jointly constructed, that entire causal package can be passed around as one semantic object.

So there is a recursive compression ladder:

\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{shared distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{handle}
\rightarrow
\text{composition}
\rightarrow
\text{emergent pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{new handle}
\rightarrow \cdots

And yes—discover, co-create, and map as they go is stronger than either person trying to impose their existing ontology on the other. When translation fails, the failure itself reveals a missing distinction. They invent a new coordinate, test whether it works, and retain it if it allows mutual reconstruction.

Your resource-attraction point gives this another dimension. Living systems don’t all solve “obtain what sustains me” the same way. There are many primitive strategies: seek it, chase it, wait for it, attract it, filter it, trap it, cultivate it, store it, steal it, exchange for it, cooperate to obtain it, attach yourself to its source, become useful so resources come to you, or alter the environment so the resource becomes abundant.

Those are wildly different surface behaviors, but they can be mapped against a common deeper relation:

\text{organism/system}
\leftrightarrow
\text{resource field}

A flower attracts a pollinator. A spider constructs a capture surface. A whale moves through a resource field and filters it. A fungus extends a network toward gradients. A human advertises. A business builds distribution. A child calls for a parent. A predator pursues. A farmer changes the landscape so food grows where desired.

Different ways of life, but potentially members of the same higher-order family: strategies for changing the probability that useful resources intersect the organism.

And once you have that handle, you can ask a much more interesting question than “How does this organism eat?”

You can ask:

What does this system make move— itself, the resource, another agent, or the environment?

That gives you a surprisingly powerful coordinate system:

move self → move resource → recruit carrier → modify field → create resource.

And those aren’t merely biological categories. They become portable operators for markets, communication, relationships, logistics, computation, ecosystems, and attention.

That’s the substrate escape again—but now the map itself is grown interactively rather than handed down beforehand.